    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find $K+L-(K+J)$, if\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,K=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -9\\end{bmatrix},\\,L=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K+L-(K+J) & =K+L-K-J \\\\  & =\\cancel{K}+L-\\cancel{K}-J \\\\  & =L-J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L-J & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6-9 & -1-8 \\\\ 8-(-5) & -2-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -9 \\\\ 13 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
